    "title": "Process masquerading as a system binary",
    "desc": "A program was run from a world-writable scratch dir (/tmp, /dev/shm, /var/tmp) under the name of a core system daemon (sshd, crond, systemd, kworker ...), or a shell was copied into such a disguise, or a process ran out of a directory named \"...\" (a hidden-in-plain-sight masquerade). Renaming a payload to look like a trusted process, or hiding it behind a dotted directory, is how an attacker blends into `ps` output (MITRE T1036.005). A real system daemon lives in /usr/bin, /usr/sbin or the nix store, never in a scratch dir, so a daemon-named binary in /tmp is the signal.",
